me('mp', DOCS_modelChunkLoadStart - DOCS_modelChunkParseStart); DOCS_warmStartDocumentLoader.loadModelChunk(DOCS_modelChunk); DOCS_modelChunk = undefined;</script><script nonce="DdGKMlUoUR2Jb_2wFrCmKQ">DOCS_modelChunkParseStart = new Date().getTime();</script><script nonce="DdGKMlUoUR2Jb_2wFrCmKQ">DOCS_modelChunk = {"chunk":[{"ty":"is","ibi":4456,"s":"The global taxonomic infrastructure operates under increasing strain. Global biodiversity is declining at an unprecedented rate (Keck et al. 2025), intensifying the need for accurate species identification and tracking, while biological data are being generated, digitized, and aggregated at unprecedented scales (Bayraktarov et al., 2019; Heberling et al., 2021; Ji et al., 2026; GBIF No. 12, 2025). Occurrence records, genomic sequences, ecological networks, and host–pathogen databases – to give some examples - are increasingly integrated across global repositories such as the Global Biodiversity Information Facility (GBIF), GenBank, BOLD, and institutional collections. The reliability of these systems depends on the assumption that taxonomic names consistently refer to comparable biological entities. Increasingly, that assumption does not hold.\nA central but often overlooked issue is that a name string - the sequence of letters used to represent the name of a biological taxon (e.g., Homo sapiens) - is not equivalent to the taxonomic species concept it represents. Taxonomic names are shorthand for evolving hypotheses about species boundaries and evolutionary relationships (Zachos, 2018). The same binomial may refer to different circumscriptions – the set of populations included in a taxon - across taxonomic classifications or time, while different names may refer to the same lineage. Advances in integrative taxonomy increasingly combine molecular, morphological, ecological, behavioural, and biogeographic evidence to refine species boundaries and classifications (Dayrat, 2005; Padial et al., 2010; Solari et al., 2019). As a result, species concepts are continually reassessed as new evidence emerges (de Queiroz, 2025; Zachos, 2018). Differences in synonymy, rank, genus placement, spelling, or species delimitation therefore reflect scientific progress, yet biodiversity data systems frequently treat names as stable identifiers (Page 2008). Without explicit tracking of taxonomic species concepts, including who defined them, when, and under what circumscription, data repositories implicitly assume stability. As a result, records may be combined or split based solely on name matching, rather than if they represent the same biological concept.\nBecause scientific names underpin biological databases, museum collections, scientific publications, genomic resources, and conservation assessments, changes in species circumscriptions can propagate throughout interconnected biological infrastructures and downstream applications (Figure 1).\n\n*\nFigure 1: Downstream Effects of Taxonomic Changes: Why taxonomic concept alignment matters. (a) Historically, many species were recognised as single, widespread taxonomic concepts. The bent-winged bat Miniopterus schreibersii is used here as an illustrative example of a species that was once thought to encompass a broad geographic distribution. (b) As scientific knowledge advances, taxonomic revisions can divide historical concepts into multiple species with distinct distributions and biological identities. Although scientific names may persist, the biological entities they represent can change substantially through time. (c) This creates challenges across biological data because historical records and downstream applications are typically linked only by scientific names, making it unclear which contemporary species concept those records actually represent. (d) Taxonomic concept alignment, such as the Bat Taxonomic Alignment (BTA) framework presented here, addresses this challenge by explicitly linking historical and contemporary taxonomic concepts throug