match; Rees, 2014), web-based resolution services (e.g., TNRS; Boyle et al., 2013), multi-database query frameworks (Berendsohn et al. 2026), and phylogeny matching tools (e.g., Phylomatcher; Radar et al., 2023). These approaches are effective for correcting misspellings, resolving orthographic variants, and identifying synonyms, but they operate primarily at the level of name strings rather than dynamic taxonomic concepts (Tsang et al., 2016). Consequently, they do not resolve deeper incongruences arising from alternative species circumscriptions, rank changes, or conflicting taxonomic treatments (Franz et al., 2016; Patterson et al., 2010). Large biodiversity infrastructures such as GBIF maintain extensive synonymy and cross-reference records, yet their effective use still requires manual interpretation to determine which taxonomic concept a name actually represents. \nEven in the largest data repositories, significant ingestion gaps persist (Garnett, 2020). When aggregators attempt to harmonize data from hundreds of sources, they frequently adopt a single preferred taxonomic treatment (Mesibov, 2013; 2018; Franz et al., 2016). As demonstrated by Mesibov (2013, 2018), automated look-up tables often prioritize string-to-string matching over taxonomic concepts, potentially overwriting the original taxonomic intent of source datasets. Consequently, records may be reassigned to accepted names that reflect different taxonomic concepts or an outdated circumscription (Burgin et al., 2018). Without provenance or concept-level tracking, it becomes difficult for researchers to determine whether a name reflects a contemporary taxonomic consensus or simply an artifact of an automated harmonization process.\nAs taxonomic discovery accelerates and global data integration expands, the need for transparent, versioned alignment of taxonomic concepts becomes increasingly urgent. Currently, no freely available framework provides concept-driven reconciliation of taxonomic names across taxonomic authorities while simultaneously preserving alternative interpretations, historical provenance, and the temporal and geographic context in which taxonomic decisions are made (Radar et al., 2023). \nBats (Order Chiroptera) provide an ideal system in which to develop and test a concept alignment framework. The number of recognized bat species has increased by more than one-third since the early 1990s, reflecting rapid advances in phylogenetics, integrative taxonomy, and the resolution of cryptic species complexes (Monadjem et al., 2024; Burgin et al., 2025). As illustrated in Figure 1, taxonomic revisions can substantially alter the biological meaning associated with scientific names, creating challenges for many downstream disciplines, including biodiversity monitoring, conservation assessments, ecological studies, ecosystem service evaluations, and disease surveillance. Although bats are used here as a model system, these challenges extend across biodiversity science wherever data are linked through taxonomic names that change through time.\nHere, we present the Bat Taxonomic Alignment (BTA), an open-access, version-controlled framework designed to document and align taxonomic concepts across major taxonomic authorities spanning more than four decades. Rather than acting as a new taxonomic authority, the BTA preserves and organizes how species concepts are treated across alternative classifications, explicitly recording synonymy, rank changes, genus transfers, and competing interpretations. By shifting the focus from name strings to taxonomic concepts, the BTA provides a transparent bridge among biodiversity repositories, literature, and conservation workflows. Using bats as a globally relevant case study, we demonstrate how concept-level alignment can reconcile alternative taxonomic treatments, quantify disagreement among leading taxonomic authorities, and reveal inconsistencies in conservation infrastructure through an audit of the IUCN Red List. Together, these analyses illustrate the broader value of taxonomic concept alignment"},{"ty":"iss","sugid":"suggest.pyfowyrtf06z","ibi":12894,"s":"s"},{"ty":"is","ibi":12895,"s":" for "},{"ty":"iss","sugid":"suggest.2u85m3k9zm1v","ibi":12900,"s":"accurate and "},{"ty":"is","ibi":12913,"s":"reproducible biodiversity science, conservation"},{"ty":"iss","sugid":"suggest.bizri0m4tn56","ibi":12960,"s":" priorities"},{"ty":"is","ibi":12971,"s":", and biodiversity data integration in an era of rapid taxonomic change.\n"},{"ty":"as","st":"paragraph","si":8499,"ei":8499,"sm":{"ps_klt_i":true,"ps_awao_i":true,"ps_ls":1.5,"ps_sm_i":true,"ps_ls_i":false,"ps_sa":12.0,"ps_il_i":true,"ps_sb":12.0,"ps_ir_i":true,"ps_al_i":false,"ps_bl_i":true,"ps_sd_i":true,"ps_sb_i":false,"ps_sa_i":false,"ps_lslm_i":true,"ps_br_i":true,"ps_bbtw_i":true,"ps_kwn_i":true,"ps_bt_i":true,"ps_al":3,"ps_ifl_i":true,"ps_bb_i":true,"ps_pbb_i":true}},{"ty":"as","st":"paragraph","si":9701,"ei":9701,"sm":{"ps_klt_i":true,"ps_awao_i":true,"ps_ls":1.5,"ps_sm_i":true,"ps_ls_i":false,"ps_sa":12.0,"ps_il_i":true,"ps_sb":12.0,"ps_ir_i":true,"ps_al_i":false,"ps_bl_i":true,"ps_sd_i":true,"ps_sb_i":false,"ps_sa_i":false,"ps_lslm_i":true,"ps_br_i":true,"ps_bbtw_i":true,"ps_kwn_i":true,"ps_bt_i":true,"ps_al":3,"ps_ifl_i":true,"ps_bb_i":true,"ps_pbb_i":true}},{"ty":"as","st":"paragraph","si":10543,"ei":10543,"sm":{"ps_klt_i":true,"ps_awao_i":true,"ps_ls":1.5,"ps_sm_i":true,"ps_ls_i":false,"ps_sa":12.0,"ps_il_i":true,"ps_sb":12.0,"ps_ir_i":true,"ps_al_i":false,"ps_bl_i":true,"ps_sd_i":true,"ps_sb_i":false,"ps_sa_i":false,"ps_lslm_i":true,"ps_br_i":true,"ps_bbtw_i":true,"ps_kwn_i":true,"ps_bt_i":true,"ps_al":3,"ps_ifl_i":true,"ps_bb_i":true,"ps_pbb_i":true}},{"ty":"as","st":"paragraph","si":11025,"ei":11025,"sm":{"ps_klt_i":true,"ps_awao_i":true,"ps_ls":1.5,"ps_sm_i":true,"ps_ls_i":false,"ps_sa":12.0,"ps_il_i":true,"ps_sb":12.0,"ps_ir_i":true,"ps_al_i":false,"ps_bl_i":true,"ps_sd_i":true,"ps_sb_i":false,"ps_sa_i":false,"ps_lslm