nomic concepts to be linked across 45 years of bat systematics. The resulting framework preserves provenance, documents concept relationships, and supports FAIR (Findable, Accessible, Interoperable, and Reusable) dissemination through versioned releases on Zenodo.\n\nTaxonomic scope and authority coverage\nThe Bat Taxonomic Alignment (BTA) was developed as a proof-of-concept framework for documenting and reconciling taxonomic concepts across changing classifications and through time. Bats provide an ideal system in which to develop and test such a framework because they have experienced extensive taxonomic revision over recent decades, resulting in substantial changes to species circumscriptions across major taxonomic authorities. Because bats underpin research and decision-making across fields ranging from conservation and biodiversity monitoring to disease ecology and public health, these changes can have far-reaching consequences. Although "},{"ty":"iss","sugid":"suggest.xk07z5dd7c62","ibi":14960,"s":"the "},{"ty":"is","ibi":14964,"s":"BTA "},{"ty":"iss","sugid":"suggest.fugee9a4sg21","ibi":14968,"s":"was"},{"ty":"is","ibi":14971,"s":"is developed using bats as a model system, the underlying challenge of reconciling changing taxonomic concepts extends throughout biodiversity science.\nTo capture this dynamic history, the BTA incorporates taxonomic treatments from "},{"ty":"iss","sugid":"suggest.xxki1ef1c8ez","ibi":15203,"s":"ten"},{"ty":"is","ibi":15206,"s":"fifteen major authorities"},{"ty":"iss","sugid":"suggest.q15g5sxdv6xx","ibi":15231,"s":", some represented by multiple versions,"},{"ty":"is","ibi":15271,"s":" spanning 45 years (1980–2025; Table 1). Authorities were selected to represent major milestones in the development of bat taxonomy, including globally recognized taxonomic references, conservation taxonomies, and contemporary digital taxonomic resources. These include successive editions of Mammal Species of the World (MSW), Handbook of the Mammals of the World (HMW), BatNames, the Mammal Diversity Database (MDD), and the IUCN Red List, together with additional authorities that have played important roles in shaping contemporary bat taxonomy. Collectively, these works provide a chronological record of changing species circumscriptions, synonymies, genus assignments, and higher-level classifications, documenting a period during which recognized bat diversity increased from fewer than 1,000 species in the early 1980s to 1,498 species in 2025.\n\nTable 1: Taxonomic authorities incorporated into the Bat Taxonomic Alignment (BTA). Chronological overview of the fifteen taxonomic authorities incorporated into the BTA, spanning 1980–2025. Together, these resources document the transition from historical printed classifications to contemporary versioned digital taxonomies and capture a period during which recognized bat diversity increased from 950 to 1,498 species.\n \n\u0010\u0012\u001cTaxonomic Authority \n\u001cFormat\n\u001cAcronym\n\u001cYear Published\n\u001cValid Species Count\n\u001cCitation\n\u001c\n\u001c\n\u0012\u001cA World List of Mammalian Species\n\u001cprinted\n\u001cCH1_1980\n\u001c1980\n\u001c950\n\u001cCorbet and Hill 1980\n\u001c\n\u001c\n\u0012\u001cMammal Species of the World: A Taxonomic and Geographic Reference\n\u001cprinted\n\u001cMSW1_1982\n\u001c1982\n\u001c928\n\u001cHonacki et al., 1982\n\u001c\n\u001c\n\u0012\u001cA World List of Mammalian Species 3rd ed.\n\u001cprinted\n\u001cCH3_1991\n\u001c1991\n\u001c977\n\u001cCorbet and Hill 1991\n\u001c\n\u001c\n\u0012\u001cMammal Species of the World: A Taxonomic and Geographic Reference. 2nd ed.: Order Chiroptera\n\u001cprinted\n\u001cMSW2_1993\n\u001c1993\n\u001c916\n\u001cKoopman 1993\n\u001c\n\u001c\n\u0012\u001cChiroptera: Systematics. Handbook of Zoology. Volume VIII Mammalia.\n\u001cprinted\n\u001cKoopman_1994\n\u001c1994\n\u001c908\n\u001cKoopman 1994\n\u001c\n\u001c\n\u0012\u001cMammal Species of the World\n\u001cprinted\n\u001cMSW3_2005\n\