17833,"ei":17836}],"revision":1249}; DOCS_modelChunkLoadStart = new Date().getTime(); DOCS_initialLoadTiming.incrementTime('mp', DOCS_modelChunkLoadStart - DOCS_modelChunkParseStart); DOCS_warmStartDocumentLoader.loadModelChunk(DOCS_modelChunk); DOCS_modelChunk = undefined;</script><script nonce="DdGKMlUoUR2Jb_2wFrCmKQ">DOCS_modelChunkParseStart = new Date().getTime();</script><script nonce="DdGKMlUoUR2Jb_2wFrCmKQ">DOCS_modelChunk = {"chunk":[{"ty":"is","ibi":18317,"s":"The resulting framework preserves taxonomic treatments across more than four decades of bat systematics while retaining the original context"},{"ty":"iss","sugid":"suggest.cxhpx7tfw0rj","ibi":18457,"s":", citations for the publications supporting the change,"},{"ty":"is","ibi":18512,"s":" and provenance associated with each authority. By documenting how species concepts are treated across successive classifications, the BTA provides a foundation for tracking taxonomic change, identifying areas of agreement and disagreement among authorities, and linking historical and contemporary biodiversity data. Detailed descriptions of data acquisition and digitization workflows, dataset standardization procedures, and provenance preservation are provided in Supplementary 2. \n\nConcept alignment framework\nTaxonomic concepts were aligned across successive classifications using a combination of automated matching and manual expert review. The objective was not simply to reconcile name strings, but to determine how species concepts were treated across authorities and through time. The BTA aligns taxonomic concepts across successive authorities by documenting how species treatments change through time. Rather than recording only accepted names, the framework captures the broader taxonomic context associated with each concept, including subspecies, synonyms, alternative names, taxonomic notes, "},{"ty":"iss","sugid":"suggest.sgtq1oc06qxb","ibi":19622,"s":"associated literature, "},{"ty":"is","ibi":19645,"s":"and supporting treatments. This information allows concepts to be traced across classifications even when names, circumscriptions, or taxonomic interpretations change.\nTaxonomic concepts are linked using a standardized set of documented relationships that capture common forms of taxonomic change (Figure 3). These include species splits, species mergers, transfers, spelling changes and nomina dubia. Newly described species represent taxa formally recognized as new to science, whereas species splits and mergers reflect changes in species circumscriptions. Transfers record movement of taxa within the taxonomic hierarchy, typically between genera or higher-level groups. Spelling changes capture orthographic corrections and nomenclatural standardizations, while nomina dubia represent names of uncertain application. Together, these relationships provide a consistent framework for documenting how taxonomic concepts evolve through time.\n\n*\nFigure 3. Taxonomic concept relationships recorded within the Bat Taxonomic Alignment (BTA). The BTA documents how species concepts change across taxonomic authorities and through time using a standardized set of concept relationships. Illustrated examples include newly described species, species splits, species mergers, transfers, spelling changes, and nomina dubia. By explicitly recording these relationships, the framework links historical and contemporary taxonomic treatments while preserving provenance and the context associated with taxonomic decisions. Bat silhouettes were obtained from PhyloPic.\n\nA key feature of the BTA is the preservation of provenance. Rather than replacing historical classifications with a single preferred taxonomy, the framework retains authority-specific treatments together with their associated taxonomic notes, source citations, and supporting information. Complete verbatim treatments are preserved within the dataset, enabling users to examine the original context in which "},{"ty":"iss","sugid":"suggest.pt70p2gdfd9y","ibi":21610,"s":"alignment"},{"ty":"is","ibi":21619,"s":"taxonomic decisions were made and to trace the evidence supporting changes in species circumscriptions through time. This allows users to determine, for example, when a species concept was first recognized, which historical concepts contributed to a contemporary species, how different authorities interpreted species limits, and what taxonomic evidence or geographic scope underpinned those decisions. By maintaining direct links to the original treatments, the BTA provides a transparent record of taxonomic reasoning rather than only the final taxonomic outcome.\nThe resulting framework functions as an alignment layer among taxonomic authorities rather than a replacement taxonomy. By documenting concept relationships, preserving provenance, and retaining alternative names and treatments, the BTA enables biodiversity data collected under different classifications to be interpreted within an explicit taxonomic context. Detailed descriptions of concept matching procedures, relationship definitions, and manual reconciliation examples are provided in Supplementary 3.\n"},{"ty":"ae","et":"inline","id":"i.2","epm":{"ee_eo":{"eo_bo":{"ln_s":0,"ln_w":0.0,"ln_c2":{"hclr_color":"#000000","clr_type":0}},"i_pid":null,"eo_mb":9.0,"i_ht":71.0,"eo_rtdf":{"rdf_ft":0},"eo_ml":9.0,"eo_mr":9.0,"eo_ad":null,"i_cid":"s-blob-v1-IMAGE-ewIEiDR4EJc","i_cont":0.0,"eo_mt":9.0,"eo_type":0,"eo_lco":{"lc_oi":"","lc_cs":"","lc_ct":0,"lc_sci":"","lc_srk":""},"i_wth":468.0,"eo_at":null,"i_iwc":{"iwc_w":false},"eo_hb":false,"eo_rtd":"","i_crop":{"crop_oxr":-0.0,"crop_oyr":-0.0,"crop_rot":0.0,"crop_hr":1.0,"crop_wr":1.0},"i_iw":false,"i_rot":0.0,"i_clst":{"cv":{"op":"set","opValue":[]}},"i_bri":0.0,"i_msct":0,"i_src":null,"i_opa":1.0}}},{"ty":"te","id":"i.2","spi":20589},{"ty":"as","st":"paragraph","si":18997,"ei":18997,"sm":{"ps_klt_i":true,"ps_awao_i":true,"ps_ls":1.5,"ps_sm_i":true,"ps_ls_i":false,"ps_sa":12.0,"ps_il_i":true,"ps_sb":1