t.pt70p2gdfd9y","si":21619,"ei":21628}],"revision":1249}; DOCS_modelChunkLoadStart = new Date().getTime(); DOCS_initialLoadTiming.incrementTime('mp', DOCS_modelChunkLoadStart - DOCS_modelChunkParseStart); DOCS_warmStartDocumentLoader.loadModelChunk(DOCS_modelChunk); DOCS_modelChunk = undefined;</script><script nonce="DdGKMlUoUR2Jb_2wFrCmKQ">DOCS_modelChunkParseStart = new Date().getTime();</script><script nonce="DdGKMlUoUR2Jb_2wFrCmKQ">DOCS_modelChunk = {"chunk":[{"ty":"is","ibi":22694,"s":"\nResource format and accessibility\nThe BTA is distributed as a version-controlled, open-access resource on Zenodo (Sherman et al., 2025) in both human-readable and machine-readable formats. It can be accessed at: "},{"ty":"iss","sugid":"suggest.fr375zoo4pw6","ibi":22907,"s":"https://zenodo.org/records/16423149"},{"ty":"is","ibi":22942,"s":"XXX. Each "},{"ty":"iss","sugid":"suggest.wzmy2zj3gpmb","ibi":22952,"s":"version "},{"ty":"is","ibi":22960,"s":"release receives a persistent DOI, enabling users to cite the exact version used in analyses while ensuring that previous versions remain permanently accessible.\nThe BTA is a spreadsheet organized around species concepts and consists of four primary components: (i) Valid Names, an interpreted index of species concepts across authorities; (ii) Full Treatments, which preserve verbatim authority-specific taxonomic accounts and supporting notes; (iii) Alternative Names, a comprehensive index of synonyms, subspecies, misspellings, abbreviations, and historical name variants; and (iv) concept relationship tables documenting how species concepts change across authorities and through time. An overview of the column layout and contents is summarized in Table 2.\n\f\nTable 2. Overview of the principal components and fields contained within the Bat Taxonomic Alignment (BTA). This table describes the structure of the BTA and the fields used to record taxonomic concepts, authority-specific treatments, concept relationships, provenance, and alternative names.\n\u0010\u0012\u001cBTA Columns\n\u001cBrief explanation of column contents\n\u0012\u001cA-O \n\u001cvalid names according to each of the fifteen taxonomic authorities\n\u0012\u001cQ \n\u001cTaxonomic Notes (Concatenated) - Consolidated taxonomic notes extracted from source treatments, including supporting rationale, citations, and authority identifiers (e.g. [HMW]) to preserve provenance and facilitate rapid interpretation of taxonomic decisions.\n\u0012\u001cS-AL\n\u001cSubspecies and Synonyms – Subspecies and synonyms reported by the source authority. Subspecies are shown in bold and synonyms in italics following MSW conventions; names following a bolded subspecies indicate synonyms associated with that subspecies.\n\u0012\u001cAN-AQ\n\u001cFull verbatim species treatment (Corbet and Hill 1980)\n\u0012\u001cAH-AY\n\u001cFull verbatim species treatment (Honacki et al., 1982)\n\u0012\u001cAZ-BC\n\u001cFull verbatim species treatment (Corbet and Hill 1991)\n\u0012\u001cBD-BK\n\u001cFull verbatim species treatment (Koopman 1993)\n\u0012\u001cBL-CC\n\u001cFull verbatim species treatment (Koopman 1994)\n\u0012\u001cCD-DA\n\u001cFull verbatim species treatment (Wilson \u0026 Reeder 2005)\n\u0012\u001cDB-EA\n\u001cFull verbatim species treatment (Wilson \u0026 Mittermeier 2019)\n\u0012\u001cEB-ES\n\u001cFull verbatim species treatment (Simmons and Cirranello 2022)\n\u0012\u001cET-GM\n\u001cFull verbatim species treatment (Mammal Diversity Database 2022)\n\u0012\u001cGN-HV\n\u001cFull verbatim species treatment (IUCN 2022)\n\u0012\u001cHW-IM\n\u001cFull verbatim species treatment (Simmons and Cirranello 2023)\n\u0012\u001cIN-KK\n\u001cFull verbatim species treatment (Mammal Diversity Database 2023)\n\u0012\u001cKL-MJ\n\u001cFull verbatim species treatment (Mammal Diversity Database 2025a)\n\u0012\u001cMK-NC\n\u001cFull verbatim species treatment (Simmons and Cirranello 2025)\n\u0012\u001cND-PD\n\u001cFull verbatim species treatment (Mammal Diversity Database 2025b)\n\u0012\u001cPE\n\u001cConcatenated list of all alternative names\n\u0012\u001cPF-TF\n\u001cAgreement Index\n\u0011\nTogether, these components enable users to track taxonomic concepts across 45 years of bat systematics, trace the provenance of taxonomic decisions, identify alternative treatments, and reconcile biodiversity data collected under different classifications. The resource is accompanied by documentation, version histories, and a video walkthrough, all hosted on Zenodo, to facilitate reuse across biodiversity informatics, conservation, ecological research, and related applications.\nTo maximize interoperability, the BTA follows FAIR (Findable, Accessible, Interoperable, and Reusable; Wilkinson et al., 2016) data principles and is designed as a living resource that can be updated as new taxonomic evidence emerges and additional authorities become available.\n\n\n\n\f\nApplications of the BTA\nA primary application of the BTA is the reconciliation of biodiversity data collected under different taxonomic classifications. By linking taxonomic concepts across successive authorities, the framework enables users to determine how a species was treated at a particular point in time and how that treatment relates to current taxonomy. This allows historical records, publications, museum collections, conservation assessments, field guides, and biodiversity databases to be interpreted within their original taxonomic context while remaining connected to contemporary classifications. \n"},{"ty":"as","st":"paragraph","si":22694,"ei":22694,"sm":{"ps_klt_i":true,"ps_awao_i":true,"ps_ls":1.5,"ps_sm_i":true,"ps_ls_i":false,"ps_sa":12.0,"ps_il_i":true,"ps_sb":12.0,"ps_ir_i":true,"ps_al_i":false,"ps_bl_i":true,"ps_sd_i":true,"ps_sb_i":false,"ps_sa_i":false,"ps_lslm_i":true,"ps_br_i":true,"ps_bbtw_i":true,"ps_kwn_i":true,"ps_bt_i":true,"ps_al":3,"ps_ifl_i":true,"ps_bb_i":true,"ps_pbb_i":true}},{"ty":"as","st":"paragraph","si":22728,"ei":22728,"sm":{"ps_klt_i":true,"ps_a