en 1980 and 2025, creating a chronological record of changing species concepts and taxonomic treatments in Chiroptera.\nTo quantify agreement among authorities, we calculated an Agreement Index based on pairwise comparisons of aligned taxonomic concepts. For each epithet, authorities were scored as either concordant (0) or discordant (1) in their treatment of the taxon. Disagreement was recorded when authorities differed in their interpretation of a concept, for example when one authority recognized a taxon as a valid species while another treated it as a synonym, subspecies, or alternative taxonomic concept. Summing these pairwise differences provided a measure of taxonomic incongruence among authorities over time, which we visualized as a heatmap (Figure 4).\n*\nFigure 4. Taxonomic disagreement among 15 bat taxonomic authorities spanning 1980–2025. Values indicate the number of aligned taxonomic concepts receiving different treatments between pairs of authorities. Higher values indicate greater disagreement in species circumscriptions, synonymies, or taxonomic placement. The heatmap reveals decreasing divergence among recent authorities, but persistent disagreement between the IUCN Red List and contemporary taxonomic authorities, motivating the audit presented in Application 2.\n\nAlthough many species concepts were treated consistently across authorities, substantial disagreement was observed among several modern classifications. For example, 349 disagreements were identified between HMW (2019) and MDD v.2.2 (2025), reflecting extensive recent taxonomic revision. Comparisons involving the IUCN Red List revealed 443 disagreements with BatNames 2025 and 451 disagreements with MDD v.2.2 (2025), highlighting the challenges of maintaining alignment among rapidly evolving taxonomic resources. The BTA identified numerous cases in which identical or similar name strings were associated with different taxonomic concepts across authorities. For example, the IUCN Red List recognizes Lasiurus salinae as a valid species, whereas HMW treats the taxon as Lasiurus blossevillii salinae, reflecting a subspecies-level interpretation. Although the underlying epithet is shared, the taxonomic concepts represented by these treatments differ.\nTogether, these results demonstrate that while broad agreement exists across much of bat taxonomy, important areas of disagreement persist among major authorities. By explicitly aligning concepts rather than names, the BTA makes these discrepancies visible and traceable through time.\n\nExample application 2: Auditing taxonomic consistency within conservation infrastructure\nThe IUCN Red List is among the most widely used biodiversity resources globally and serves as the taxonomic foundation for many conservation assessments, policy decisions, funding mechanisms, and biodiversity databases ("},{"ty":"iss","sugid":"suggest.hywzxzg3qdtg","ibi":30597,"s":"Rodrigues et al. 2006 "},{"ty":"is","ibi":30619,"s":"CITE CITE). Because taxonomic concepts continue to change through time, maintaining alignment between conservation infrastructure and contemporary taxonomic authorities represents an ongoing challenge ("},{"ty":"iss","sugid":"suggest.e4b2iczbbpkm","ibi":30821,"s":"Gotelli, 2004; Savage, 1995"},{"ty":"is","ibi":30848,"s":"CITE CITE).\nTo demonstrate the utility of the BTA for taxonomic auditing, in September 2024 we audited bat taxonomy within the IUCN Red List by comparing species concepts recognized by the IUCN Red List with those recognized by the Mammal Diversity Database (MDD) v2.2 (2025), the most recent global mammal taxonomic authority available at the time of the audit. \n"},{"ty":"ae","et":"inline","id":"kix.gnf4yda3vqt4","epm":{"ee_eo":{"eo_bo":{"ln_s":0,"ln_w":0.0,"ln_c2":{"hclr_color":"#000000","clr_type":0}},"i_pid":null,"eo_mb":9.0,"i_ht":423.0,"eo_rtdf":{"rdf_ft":0},"eo_ml":9.0,"eo_mr":9.0,"eo_ad":null,"i_cid":"s-blob-v1-IMAGE-TWWGlaeGrVg","i_cont":0.0,"eo_mt":9.0,"eo_type":0,"eo_lco":{"lc_oi"