in the IUCN Red List, representing ~20% of assessed bat species. The discrepancies spanned multiple categories of taxonomic change, including species splits, newly recognized species absent from IUCN records, generic reassignments, spelling corrections, species mergers, and rank changes (Figure 5).\n*\nFigure 5. Categories of taxonomic modifications identified during an audit of bat taxonomy within the IUCN Red List. Comparison of IUCN Red List taxonomic treatments with Mammal Diversity Database (MDD) "},{"ty":"iss","sugid":"suggest.omf9s37rf2kf","ibi":31832,"s":"iteratively refined from the BTA"},{"ty":"is","ibi":31864,"s":"v2.2 (2025) identified 287 modifications. These discrepancies illustrate how differences among taxonomic authorities can accumulate within downstream conservation infrastructure.\n\nOne illustrative example involves the Myotis nattereri species complex. While recent authorities such as BatNames and the Mammal Diversity Database recognize several distinct species within this complex, including Myotis hoveli, M. crypticus, M. escalerai, and M. zenatius (e.g., Çoraman et al., 2019), the IUCN Red List continues to retain a broader historical circumscription. Such differences alter the biological entities being assessed and may influence interpretations of geographic range size, conservation status, and management priorities.\nThe resulting audit was compiled into a detailed report and submitted to the IUCN bat specialist community for review. In addition to identifying discrepancies, the report provided supporting taxonomic notes, concept relationships, literature references, provenance information, and recommended actions for each affected record. By linking historical and contemporary species concepts within a transparent framework, the BTA enables large-scale taxonomic audits that would otherwise require extensive manual reconciliation. This application demonstrates how concept-level taxonomic alignment can be used not only to compare taxonomic authorities but also to evaluate the consistency of biodiversity infrastructure that depends upon them.\nDiscussion\nWe developed the Bat Taxonomic Alignment to reconcile taxonomic concepts across "},{"ty":"iss","sugid":"suggest.g79xqaby69r4","ibi":33422,"s":"ten "},{"ty":"is","ibi":33426,"s":"fifteen authorities spanning 45 years of bat systematics and to evaluate how changing taxonomies influence biodiversity data through time. Our analyses demonstrate that agreement among taxonomic authorities cannot be assumed, even within a comparatively well-studied mammalian order. Although scientific names are often treated as stable identifiers, the BTA revealed substantial differences in how authorities circumscribe species concepts, assign synonymies, and recognize taxonomic boundaries, with hundreds of conflicting species treatments persisting among contemporary classifications despite increasing efforts toward taxonomic standardization. Application of the framework to the IUCN Red List further identified 287 taxonomic modifications affecting ~20% of assessed bat species, demonstrating how differences among taxonomic authorities can propagate into biodiversity infrastructure. Together, these results highlight a fundamental challenge facing modern biodiversity science: scientific names are frequently used as proxies for biological entities, yet the taxonomic concepts they represent can change substantially through time. By explicitly aligning taxonomic concepts rather than names alone, the BTA makes these changes visible, traceable, and reproducible.\nAlthough demonstrated here using bats, the challenges identified by this study are not unique to Chiroptera. Similar disagreements among competing taxonomic authorities occur across many groups of organisms, including birds, reptiles, amphibians, plants, fungi, insects, and microbial taxa, where alternative classifications frequently coexist and species circumscriptions continue to evolve ("},{"ty":"iss","sugid":"suggest.nav17brrafiz","ibi":35095,"s":"Vences et al., 2013"},{"ty":"is","ibi":35114,"s":"ADD CITATIONS). Anyone working with biodiversity information across long time periods - including researchers, conservation organizations, government agencies, and consultants - must therefore contend with the fact that a scientific name may refer to different biological entities depending on the authority consulted and the date at which a classification was adopted. The resulting ambiguity complicates efforts to integrate biodiversity information across datasets, institutions, and time periods, particularly when historical records are interpreted using contemporary taxonomic frameworks.\n"},{"ty":"ae","et":"inline","id":"i.4","epm":{"ee_eo":{"eo_bo":{"ln_s":0,"ln_w":0.0,"ln_c2":{"hclr_color":"#000000","clr_type":0}},"i_pid":null,"eo_mb":0.0,"i_ht":206.7244094488189,"eo_rtdf":{"rdf_ft":0},"eo_ml":0.0,"eo_mr":0.0,"eo_ad":null,"i_cid":"s-blob-v1-IMAGE-JpFwIhLm8iQ","i_cont":0.0,"eo_mt":0.0,"eo_type":0,"eo_lco":{"lc_oi":"","lc_cs":"","lc_ct":0,"lc_sci":"","lc_srk":""},"i_wth":472.6226771653543,"eo_at":null,"i_iwc":{"iwc_w":false},"eo_hb":false,"eo_rtd":"","i_crop":{"crop_oxr":-0.0,"crop_oyr":-0.0,"crop_rot":0.0,"crop_hr":1.0,"crop_wr":1.0},"i_iw":false,"i_rot":0.0,"i_clst":{"cv":{"op":"set","opValue":[]}},"i_bri":0.0,"i_msct":0,"i_src":null,"i_opa":1.0}}},{"ty":"te","id":"i.4","spi":31627},{"ty":"as","st":"paragraph","si":31626,"ei":31626,"sm":{"ps_klt_i":true,"ps_awao_i":true,"ps_ls":1.5,"ps_sm_i":true,"ps_ls_i":false,"ps_sa":12.0,"ps_il_i":true,"ps_sb":12.0,"ps_ir_i":true,"ps_al_i":false,"ps_bl_i":true,"ps_sd_i":true,"ps_sb_i":false,"ps_sa_i":false,"ps_lslm_i":true,"ps_br_i":true,"ps_bbtw_i":true,"ps_kwn_i":true,"ps_bt_i":true,"ps_al":3,"ps_ifl_i":true,"ps_bb_i":true,"ps_pbb_i":true}},{"ty":"as","st":"paragraph","si":31628,"ei":31628,"sm":{"ps_klt_i":true,"ps_awao_i":true,"ps_ls":1.5,"ps_sm_i":true,"ps_ls_i":false,"ps_sa":12.0,"ps_il_i":true,"ps_sb":12.0,"ps_ir_i":true,"ps_al_i":false,"ps_bl_