,"ei":35126}],"revision":1249}; DOCS_modelChunkLoadStart = new Date().getTime(); DOCS_initialLoadTiming.incrementTime('mp', DOCS_modelChunkLoadStart - DOCS_modelChunkParseStart); DOCS_warmStartDocumentLoader.loadModelChunk(DOCS_modelChunk); DOCS_modelChunk = undefined;</script><script nonce="DdGKMlUoUR2Jb_2wFrCmKQ">DOCS_modelChunkParseStart = new Date().getTime();</script><script nonce="DdGKMlUoUR2Jb_2wFrCmKQ">DOCS_modelChunk = {"chunk":[{"ty":"is","ibi":35709,"s":"Our IUCN audit illustrates how these discrepancies can propagate into downstream biodiversity infrastructure. As shown by the modifications identified between the IUCN Red List and MDD v2.2 (2025), taxonomic divergence is not restricted to specialist taxonomic resources but can persist within systems that underpin conservation planning, policy development, funding allocation, and biodiversity monitoring ("},{"ty":"iss","sugid":"suggest.hbzvqg6xxuk7","ibi":36117,"s":"Mace, 2004"},{"ty":"is","ibi":36127,"s":"CITE CITE). Many discrepancies reflected differences in species circumscriptions, including species splits, newly recognized taxa, and alternative interpretations of species limits, thereby altering the biological entities being assessed and potentially influencing estimates of geographic range size, population status, extinction risk, and conservation priority ("},{"ty":"iss","sugid":"suggest.j7uypt4bpiy8","ibi":36492,"s":"Mace, 2004"},{"ty":"is","ibi":36502,"s":"CITE CITE).\nThe patterns identified in the IUCN audit closely mirror the conceptual framework presented in Figure 1. When a historical species concept is subsequently divided into multiple descendant taxa, biodiversity records, conservation assessments, ecological observations, and other downstream datasets may remain linked to the historical name even though the biological entity represented by that name has changed . Such discrepancies are not merely nomenclatural; they alter the units through which biodiversity is measured, monitored, and managed. The implications extend well beyond conservation assessments (McNeely 2002; Schlick-Steiner et al. 2010; Wu et al. 2019; Almeida-Silva et al. 2021). Ecological niche models, species interaction networks, phylogenetic analyses, genomic repositories, and host–pathogen studies all depend upon accurate taxonomic attribution (Kennedy et al., 2005; Radar et al., 2023). The Miniopterus schreibersii species complex illustrates this challenge because taxonomic revision substantially altered the geographic distribution represented by the species name (Tian et al. 2004), meaning that historical pathogen records associated with the broader concept (e.g. Zheng et al., 2018) may not necessarily refer to the same biological entity recognized today. Without explicit concept alignment, such changes can obscure host distributions, affect interpretations of disease risk, and complicate comparisons among studies conducted under different taxonomic frameworks (Tsang et al., 2016).\nThe discrepancies observed here also highlight broader challenges facing contemporary taxonomic infrastructure. First, many biodiversity resources continue to operate without explicit versioning, despite its importance for retaining provenance links among taxonomic concepts, sources, and rationales (Karbstein et al. 2024). Although several initiatives have adopted version-controlled approaches, including the Mammal Diversity Database (Burgin et al., 2025), ZooBank (Pyle, 2016), Plazi (Agosti et al., 2022), and the Catalogue of Life (Garnett et al., 2020), many datasets and publications do not clearly indicate which taxonomic treatment underlies a record or analysis. The IUCN audit provides a practical example of this challenge. While updates are incorporated through Taxonomic Change Requests and expert review processes, the taxonomic backbone associated with particular assessments is not always explicitly versioned or easily reconstructed retrospectively. There are often substantial delays between the publication of taxonomic revisions and their incorporation into downstream biodiversity resources. During these intervals, newly recognized taxa may be absent from major databases or aggregated under broader taxonomic concepts, potentially obscuring biodiversity patterns and limiting discoverability in data-driven analyses (Garnett \u0026 Christidis, 2017; Troudet et al., 2018; GBIF Secretariat, 2025). Versioning is therefore essential not only for reproducibility, but also for documenting how taxonomic concepts change and propagate through biodiversity infrastructure.\nSecond, the provenance of taxonomic decisions is often difficult to trace. Many authoritative classifications are maintained by relatively small expert communities operating under substantial resource constraints that limit data sharing and transparency (Bruneman et al., 2008; Garnett, 2020; Pyle, 2016; Agosti, 2022; Ebach et al., 2011). Expert oversight remains indispensable, yet the rationale underlying taxonomic changes is not always archived in a manner that can be readily consulted by downstream users. Researchers frequently encounter updated classifications without access to the discussions, evidence, alternative treatments, or historical context that informed those decisions. Consequently, disagreements among authorities may be visible, but the reasons underlying those disagreements can remain difficult to evaluate. By preserving authority-specific treatments, taxonomic notes, literature references, and concept relationships, concept-alignment tools such as the BTA attempt to make this provenance more transparent and accessible.\n"},{"ty":"as","st":"paragraph","si":36513,"ei":36513,"sm":{"ps_klt_i":true,"ps_awao_i":true,"ps_ls":1.5,"ps_sm_i":true,"ps_ls_i":false,"ps_sa":12.0,"ps_il_i":true,"ps_sb":12.0,"ps_ir_i":true,"ps_al_i":false,"ps_bl_i":true,"ps_sd_i":true,"ps_sb_i":false,"ps_sa_i":false,"ps_lslm_i":true,"ps_br_i":true,"ps_bbtw_i":true,"ps_kwn_i":true,"ps_bt_i":true,"ps_al":3,"ps_ifl_i