e,"ts_bgc2":{"hclr_color":"#ffff00","clr_type":0},"ts_fs_i":true,"ts_ff_i":false,"ts_bgc2_i":false,"ts_it":false,"ts_va":"nor","ts_bd_i":true,"ts_va_i":true,"ts_fs":11.0,"ts_ff":"Times New Roman","ts_st":false,"ts_bd":false,"ts_fgc2_i":true,"ts_sc_i":true,"ts_tw":400}},{"ty":"msfd","sugid":"suggest.hbzvqg6xxuk7","si":36127,"ei":36135},{"ty":"msfd","sugid":"suggest.j7uypt4bpiy8","si":36502,"ei":36510},{"ty":"msfd","sugid":"suggest.8w0vvaz12d4x","si":36922,"ei":36922}],"revision":1249}; DOCS_modelChunkLoadStart = new Date().getTime(); DOCS_initialLoadTiming.incrementTime('mp', DOCS_modelChunkLoadStart - DOCS_modelChunkParseStart); DOCS_warmStartDocumentLoader.loadModelChunk(DOCS_modelChunk); DOCS_modelChunk = undefined;</script><script nonce="DdGKMlUoUR2Jb_2wFrCmKQ">DOCS_modelChunkParseStart = new Date().getTime();</script><script nonce="DdGKMlUoUR2Jb_2wFrCmKQ">DOCS_modelChunk = {"chunk":[{"ty":"is","ibi":40674,"s":"Third, access to taxonomic knowledge remains uneven. Important taxonomic references may be difficult to obtain, unavailable in machine-readable formats, or inaccessible because of financial, linguistic, geographic, or institutional barriers ("},{"ty":"iss","sugid":"suggest.3bnev83klkih","ibi":40916,"s":"Gemeinholzer et al., 2020"},{"ty":"is","ibi":40941,"s":"CITE CITE ; Karbstein et al. 2024; Cornelius et al., 2025). The incorporation of historical authorities into the BTA required substantial digitization and semantic annotation efforts, highlighting the broader challenge of mobilizing legacy taxonomic knowledge for contemporary biodiversity science. The digitization of Handbook of the Mammals of the World treatments provides one example of the effort required to transform foundational taxonomic resources into reusable digital knowledge. Similar barriers continue to limit access to the taxonomic information needed to interpret biodiversity data consistently across regions and disciplines ("},{"ty":"iss","sugid":"suggest.2yoo4zp52ui1","ibi":41585,"s":"König et al., 2019"},{"ty":"is","ibi":41603,"s":"CITE CITE).\nCollectively, these challenges suggest that biodiversity infrastructure is increasingly constrained by workflows built around static taxonomic lists. As taxonomic discovery accelerates through the integration of molecular, morphological, ecological, and biogeographic evidence, maintaining consistency across databases, conservation assessments, museum collections, and analytical pipelines requires mechanisms capable of accommodating continual revision while preserving historical context. Taxonomic updates are most useful when they are versioned, citable, machine-readable, and accompanied by sufficient detail to enable downstream users to understand and reproduce taxonomic decisions. One potential path forward is the adoption of more formalized and regularly published taxonomic update workflows, similar to those used by initiatives such as the Catalogue of Fishes ("},{"ty":"iss","sugid":"suggest.f70fia2i680b","ibi":42490,"s":"Fricke \u0026 Eschmeyer, 2026"},{"ty":"is","ibi":42514,"s":"CITE), the IOC World Bird List ("},{"ty":"iss","sugid":"suggest.bnusfzcjqeee","ibi":42546,"s":"Gill \u0026 Rasmussen, 2026."},{"ty":"is","ibi":42569,"s":"CITE), and the International Committee on Taxonomy of Viruses ("},{"ty":"iss","sugid":"suggest.o4dnzv2miura","ibi":42632,"s":"“International Committee on Taxonomy of Viruses (ICTV): https://ictv.global/taxonomy/”"},{"ty":"is","ibi":42718,"s":"CITE) where changes are documented, archived, and disseminated through versioned releases. Such approaches improve transparency, facilitate interoperability among biodiversity resources, and provide a stable foundation for tracking taxonomic concepts through time.\nThe BTA represents one approach to addressing these challenges, serving as an alignment layer among taxonomic authorities. By organizing authority-specific treatments into a structured, versioned framework, the BTA enables users to reconcile historical and contemporary classifications without losing the context in which records were originally generated. Users can determine how a species was treated by a particular authority, identify alternative names associated with a concept, trace taxonomic changes through time, and translate records collected under older classifications into contemporary taxonomic frameworks. These capabilities are particularly relevant for historical literature, museum collections, biodiversity databases, field guides, and conservation assessments that continue to rely upon classifications that may no longer reflect current understanding.\nMore broadly, the principles underlying the BTA - concept alignment, provenance preservation, versioning, and interoperability - are applicable across biodiversity science and other disciplines that rely on evolving classification systems. As biological disciplines increasingly rely upon large, interconnected datasets, taxonomic concepts must become as traceable and interoperable as occurrence records, genomic sequences, ecological observations, and conservation assessments. The challenge addressed here is therefore not fundamentally about bat taxonomy, but about ensuring that biological knowledge remains interpretable as our understanding of biodiversity continues to change. By documenting relationships among taxonomic concepts across 45 years of bat systematics, the BTA provides a practical framework for reconciling changing classifications while preserving provenance, ultimately supporting more reproducible biodiversity science, conservation assessment, and data integration.\n"},{"ty":"as","st":"paragraph","si":41614,"ei":41614,"sm":{"ps_klt_i":true,"ps_awao_i":true,"ps_ls":1.5,"ps_sm_i":true,"ps_ls_i":false,"ps_sa":12.0,"ps_il_i":true,"ps_sb":12.0,"ps_ir_i":true,"ps_al_i":false,"ps_bl_i":true,"ps_sd_i":true,"ps_sb_i":false,"ps_sa_i":false,"ps_lslm_i":true,"ps_br_i":true,"ps_bbtw_i":true,"ps_kwn_i":true,"ps_bt_i":true,"ps_al":3,"ps_ifl_i":true,"ps_bb_i":true,"ps_pbb_i":true}},{"ty