sed by a preferred taxonomic authority (his, her, or their view of the ‘right” scientific name for a taxon).\n\u001c  GNA (Global Names Architecture), 2020\n\u0012\u001cScientific name\n\u001cA name for a taxon that is formed in Latin and complies with a relevant code of nomenclature or otherwise meets the expectations of the nomenclatural and taxonomic communities. A scientific name may or may not include the names of the authors who created the name, basionym, or combination - with or without the dates of those events. The name may be annotated with terms such as subsp. (see examples under canonical name). Taxonomists establish which scientific name is the correct one to apply to a taxon, but different taxonomists may disagree in respect of the correct name. Latinized names may include authors, dates of authorship, and various annotations. ‘Parsing’ can reveal all semantic elements, scientific name can be rendered to a ‘canonical’ form of only latinized elements.\n\u001c  GNA (Global Names Architecture), 2020\n\u0012\u001cSensu\n\u001cIn the sense of; usually used to point to one person’s interpretation of a meaning.\n\u001c  GNA (Global Names Architecture), 2020\n\u0012\u001cSpecies\n\u001ca group of organisms that can be considered one taxonomic unit, typically as the lowest taxonomic rank that has an accepted name.\n\u001c Sandall et al., 2023\n\u0012\u001cSpecies concept\n\u001cthe conceptual framework for forming hypotheses that represent species. Biological or phylogenetic species concepts are examples. See also taxon concept.\n\u001c Sandall et al., 2023\n\u0012\u001cSpecies splitting and lumping\n\u001cused in the context of changing delimitation of a species due to varying taxonomic opinion, whereby one species may be divided into several species, or several species are grouped into one. This is distinct from the process of describing new species or adding new synonyms.\n\u001c Sandall et al., 2023\n\u0012\u001cSpecimen\n\u001ca physical example of an organism.\n\u001c Sandall et al., 2023\n\u0012\u001cStable identifiers\n\u001ca unique, persistent, and unchanging label assigned to a digital or physical entity (e.g., database records, biological specimens, UI components) to ensure it can be consistently referenced over time. Unlike mutable names, these identifiers—often URIs or UUIDs—remain valid despite database updates, re-indexing, or location changes.\n\u001c Consortium of European Taxonomic Facilities (CETAF), 2026\n\u0012\u001cStandards\n\u001cInformation standards are agreed conventions by which digital information can be organised. Standards are important to machine-to-machine dialog, allowing one machine to release information in a way that other machines can understand. Semanticization is dependent on standards. The primary body developing standards for biology is TDWG, but many other projects have developed in-house standards.\n\u001c  GNA (Global Names Architecture), 2020\n\u0012\u001cSynonym\n\u001can alternative name to the accepted name. In a taxonomic backbone scheme, synonyms are appended under accepted names as a ‘child’ term.\n\u001c Sandall et al., 2023\n\u0012\u001cSystematics\n\u001cThat component of the discipline of taxonomy that relates to the arrangement of taxa , normally within hierarchies or classifications.\n\u001c  GNA (Global Names Architecture), 2020\n\u0012\u001cTaxon\n\u001ca term denoting a commonly recognized unit or collective of organisms. Also called a taxonomic group.\n\u001c Sandall et al., 2023\n\u0012\u001cTaxon concept\n\u001ca set of organisms and their characteristics that form a hypothesis representing a taxon and distinguishing it from other taxa, which can vary between particular authors and can change over time with new data or specimen evidence. See also species concept.\n\u001c Sandall et al., 2023\n\u0012\u001cTaxonomic backbone\n\u001ca data structure for matching taxonomic synonyms to accepted names within a hierarchy.\n\u001c Sandall et al., 2023\n\u0012\u001cTaxonomic Classifications\n\u001c(1) the systematic grouping of items. (2) a system in which items may be grouped. (3) the arrangement of material for ease of discussion or reference, not necessarily in a manner reflecting relationships or ranks; i.e. a system (q.v.) (4) a system (q.v.) so produced, the fundamental product of taxonomy.\n\u001c Hawksworth etal., 2021\n\u0012\u001cTaxonomic name\n\u001crecognized scientific name of an organism, typically a binomial name including genus and species.\n\u001c Sandall et al., 2023\n\u0012\u001cTaxonomic treatment\n\u001cpublications or (more frequently) sections of publications documenting the features or distribution of a related group of organisms (called a “taxon”, plural “taxa”) in ways adhering to highly formalized conventions.\n\u001c Catapano, 2019\n\u0012\u001cTranscription\n\u001c(1) to copy exactly from one written work to another. (2) the rendering in written form of human speech, especially of languages employing ideographic or phonetic characters.\n\u001c Hawksworth etal., 2021\n\u0012\u001cType specimens\n\u001cA single specimen which acts as the defining reference point for the application of a name of a species or an infraspecific taxon. Type specimens are usually retained within Museums and herbaria.\n\u001c  GNA (Global Names Architecture), 2020\n\u0012\u001cValid name\n\u001cA term used by zoologists and virologists to refer to a code-compliant name of an organism. It does not mean that the name is currently endorsed as the best name for that taxon.\n\u001c  GNA (Global Names Architecture), 2020\n"}},{"ty":"nm","nmr":["ksm","t.mfl7fi6ao916"],"nmc":{"ty":"is","ibi":9358,"s":" \n\n \n\f\nSupplementary 2. Taxonomic authority acquisition and digitization\nSpecies-level circumscriptions from each authority were extracted and incorporated into the Bat Taxonomic Alignment (BTA) through a multi-stage workflow consisting of: (1) acquisition of taxonomic authorities through downloads, database exports, or digitization of printed resources; (2) conversion of authority-specific treatments into machine-readable formats; and (3) incorporation into a common framework for subsequent concept alignment and comparative analyses.\nS2.1 Acquisition of taxonomic authorities\nThe effort required to acquire taxonomic treatments varied substantially across the selected authorities. Contemporary digital resources such as the Mammal Diversity Database (MDD) were available in tabular form and required relatively little reformatting prior to incorporation into the BTA. BatNames versions were acquired directly as CSV exports from the underlying database infrastructure, with version numbers and timestamps recorded for each download when available. Access to these backend exports was facilitated by a coauthor involved in the management of BatNames. In contrast, several other authorities existed only as printed resources and required substantial effort to digitize and convert into machine-readable formats (see S2.2).\nThe IUCN Red List of Threatened Species presents a particular challenge because individual records and database releases do not currently operate under a static versioning system. Consequently, a time-stamped archival approach was required to maintain reproducibility and ensure that taxonomic audits can be reconciled with the specific version of the Red List available at the time of analysis. Documenting these temporal snapshots was essential for evaluating ingestion gaps between the IUCN Red List and other authorities such as BatNames and the Mammal Diversity Database.\nS2.2 Digitization of historical authorities and printed resources\nSeveral foundational bat taxonomic references were originally published only in print form and have subsequently been digitized through community biodiversity informatics initiatives. Historical authorities including Corbet \u0026 Hill (1980, 1991), Mammal Species of the World (MSW) editions 1 and 2, and Koopman (1994) were incorporated into the BTA through the Hesperomys database (Zijlstra, 2025a), which provides structured digital representations of historical mammal taxonomies and maintains close alignment with the Mammal Diversity Database.\nA more extensive workflow was required for Handbook of the Mammals of the World (HMW). Printed HMW volumes were digitized by Picturae, a company specializing in large-scale digitization of museum collections and scientific materials. Approximately 5,000 species treatments were converted into searchable PDF files. These PDFs were subsequently parsed and annotated using Plazi\u0027s GoldenGate mark-up editor (CITE) to generate XML files containing machine-readable descriptions of individual HMW treatments. The resulting treatments were uploaded to Plazi\u0027s Treatment Bank infrastructure and deposited as openly accessible taxonomic treatments (e.g., Burgin, 2019).\nDigitized representations of the HMW treatments were subsequently downloaded from a versioned copy of Treatment Bank (Plazi Community, 2025) and converted into comma-separated value (CSV) files using custom scripts (NEED to publish https://jhpoelen.nl/hmw as a data publication on Zenodo or similar). These structured outputs enabled direct incorporation of HMW taxonomic treatments into the BTA framework. We recognize that relatively few organizations possess the combination of resources, technical expertise, infrastructure, and funding necessary to undertake such a time-intensive and specialized digitization workflow. Nevertheless, the process transformed an important taxonomic reference into a reusable digital resource for biodiversity informatics applications and future taxonomic syntheses. HMW treatments are now openly accessible under an open-access license (Agosti \u0026 Egloff, 2009), facilitating their reuse in resources such as the BTA.\n"}},{"ty":"nm","nmr":["ksm","t.mfl7fi6ao916"],"nmc":{"ty":"as","st":"row","si":4116,"ei":4116,"sm":{"row_mh":45.0}}},{"ty":"nm","nmr":["ksm","t.mfl7fi6ao916"],"nmc":{"