s","ibi":13492,"s":" \n \n\n \n\f\nSupplementary 3. Taxonomic Concept Matching and Alignment\nS3.1 Identification of corresponding taxonomic concepts\nFollowing acquisition and digitization of taxonomic authorities (Supplementary 2), taxonomic concepts were aligned across successive classifications spanning 45 years using a combination of automated matching, synonymy information, subspecies treatments, taxonomic notes, and manual expert review.\nCustom scripts (available on GitHub; Zijlstra, 2025b) were used to parse name lists from individual authorities and match them against Hesperomys database records (Zijlstra, 2025a). Matching prioritized exact name strings but incorporated secondary heuristics, including genus-level reconciliation, where appropriate. Automated matching provided an efficient mechanism for identifying obvious correspondences among authorities but was insufficient for many taxonomic treatments that had undergone substantial revision.\nThe framework was designed to capture not only accepted species names, but also subspecies, synonyms, alternative combinations, taxonomic notes, and supporting literature. Integrating these fields enabled rapid identification of agreement among authorities, interpretation of epithet usage, and documentation of changing taxonomic concepts through time.\nWhere possible, corresponding concepts were identified through direct comparison of accepted names and synonymies. However, because species splits, mergers, rank elevations, and nomenclatural revisions are common outcomes of ongoing taxonomic research, many concepts required additional manual interpretation. Supporting taxonomic notes and published literature were therefore examined to determine how historical and contemporary concepts were related.\nS3.2 Assignment of concept relationships\nOnce corresponding concepts had been identified, relationships among taxonomic concepts were classified using the standardized categories described in Figure 2 of the main manuscript. These included newly described species, species splits, species mergers, transfers, spelling changes, nomina dubia, and alternative taxonomic treatments.\nRelationship assignments were based on evidence provided within the source authorities, including synonymies, taxonomic notes, geographic scope, and supporting literature. Species splits were recorded when a historical concept was divided into two or more contemporary taxa, whether through elevation of former subspecies, resurrection of synonyms, or recognition of previously unrecognized lineages. Species mergers were recorded when multiple previously recognized species were treated as a single taxon. Transfers captured changes in hierarchical placement, including reassignment among genera or higher taxonomic ranks. Additional categories documented nomenclatural revisions and names of uncertain application.\nSince merges, splits, and rank elevations frequently alter the status of individual epithets, current treatments were recorded within the framework using standardized indicators such as \"[synonym of]\" or by retaining trinomials where a subspecies rank remained appropriate. These annotations facilitated consistent tracking of concept relationships across authorities.\nBy explicitly recording these relationships, the BTA preserves the historical pathways through which contemporary species concepts emerged and allows users to reconstruct taxonomic change across successive authorities.\nS3.3 Complex and ambiguous alignments\nAutomated matching alone was insufficient for many taxonomic treatments. In cases where direct correspondence could not be established through name strings or synonym lists, concepts were reconciled manually using all available contextual information.\nFor example, Corynorhinus leonpaniaguae, a recently described species derived from populations formerly assigned to C. mexicanus (CITE), could not be identified automatically without interpretation of the accompanying taxonomic notes in 